from pack_writer import Writer
w=Writer('2023-june','-q001-060')
def a(n,p,o,c,h,s,t,e,status='reviewed',note=''):
 w.add(n,'Shared','Aptitude','Moderate',p,o,c,h,s,t,e,status,note)
a(1,'A and B collect ₹1, ₹2, ₹5 and ₹10 coins in the ratios 3:2:2:1 and 4:3:2:1 respectively. Each has the same total number of coins. If A’s coins are worth ₹270, what are B’s coins worth?',['₹213','₹240','₹275','₹282'],'B',['Find the value and number of coins in one ratio block.','A’s block contains 8 coins worth ₹27; B’s contains 10 coins worth ₹30.'], 'A has 10 blocks, hence 80 coins. B therefore has 8 blocks, worth 8(4+6+10+10)=₹240. Thus B is correct; ₹213, ₹275 and ₹282 do not satisfy the equal-count condition.', 'A ratio specifies relative counts, not relative total values. If denominations d_i have counts kr_i, their number is kΣr_i and their total value is kΣd_i r_i. The mean denomination is therefore Σd_i r_i/Σr_i. For two collections with equal counts, total values are in the ratio of these weighted means. The scale factors for the two ratios need not agree: a block of eight coins and a block of ten coins describe different totals. First calculate a block’s count and value, infer the actual number from the known value, and only then scale the second collection. Check that inferred counts are integral whenever individual objects are involved.', [('Coins of ₹1 and ₹5 are in ratio 3:1 and worth ₹80. Find their number.','A block has 4 coins worth ₹8. Ten blocks give 40 coins.'),('Two equal-size collections of ₹1 and ₹2 coins have ratios 1:1 and 1:3. The first is worth ₹60. Find the second’s value.','The mean values are 3/2 and 7/4. The first has 40 coins, so the second is worth 40·7/4=₹70.')])
a(2,'Increasing a train’s speed by 20% reduces the journey time by 2 hours; decreasing it by 20% increases the time by 3 hours. What is the normal duration?',['11.5 hours','12.0 hours','13.2 hours','14.0 hours'],'B',['For fixed distance, time is inversely proportional to speed.','The new times are T/1.2 and T/0.8.'],'T−T/1.2=T/6=2 gives T=12. The second condition confirms T/0.8−T=T/4=3. Only 12.0 satisfies both; 11.5, 13.2 and 14.0 give time reductions different from 2.', 'For a journey of fixed length D, the relation D=vT implies that multiplying speed by a positive factor k divides the travel time by k. Percentage changes of speed and time are consequently not equal and opposite. Increasing speed by a fraction r saves Tr/(1+r), whereas decreasing it by r adds Tr/(1−r). The latter change is larger. These formulas require the same distance and a constant effective speed; stops must either be excluded or included consistently. When two conditions are supplied, solve one and use the other as an independent consistency check. Convert percentages to fractions before manipulating the equations, and distinguish a percentage of the original time from an absolute number of hours.', [('A 25% speed increase saves 1 hour. Find the original time.','T−T/1.25=T/5=1, so T=5 hours.'),('A journey normally takes 8 hours. What happens after a 20% speed decrease?','The new time is 8/0.8=10 hours, an increase of 2 hours.')])
a(3,'A tells the truth 30% of the time and B 40% of the time, independently. What is the minimum probability that they contradict each other?',['0.18','0.42','0.46','0.50'],'C',['Contradiction is unavoidable when exactly one statement is true.','Both can lie without contradicting, so use only the two mixed cases for the minimum.'],'Independence gives P(A true,B false)=0.3·0.6=0.18 and P(A false,B true)=0.7·0.4=0.28. These disjoint events force contradiction, giving minimum 0.46, attained when both liars agree on the same false statement. A counts only one case; B is the both-false probability; D exceeds the attainable minimum.', 'Represent truth-telling by Bernoulli indicators. With independent truth probabilities p and q, the probability that exactly one person tells the truth is p(1−q)+(1−p)q. If both statements address the same fact, one true and one false statement necessarily conflict. When both lie, their statements may agree or disagree if several false answers are possible. Thus the mixed-case probability gives the minimum contradiction probability, rather than the whole contradiction probability in every setting. For a strictly binary proposition, two false answers agree and equality holds automatically. The independence assumption is essential for multiplying marginal probabilities; without it the joint distribution must be specified or bounded. Keep mutually exclusive cases separate before adding their probabilities.', [('Independent witnesses tell the truth with probabilities 0.8 and 0.7. Find minimum contradiction probability.','0.8·0.3+0.2·0.7=0.38.'),('Two independent witnesses each tell the truth half the time about a binary proposition. Find agreement probability.','Both truthful or both false: 0.5²+0.5²=0.5.')])
a(4,'The standard deviation of x₁,…,xₙ is σ>0. What is the standard deviation of 3x₁+2,…,3xₙ+2?',['3σ','σ','3σ+2','9σ'],'A',['Subtract the transformed mean from each transformed observation.','Every deviation becomes three times its original value.'],'The transformed mean is 3x̄+2, so the deviations are 3(x_i−x̄). Variance is multiplied by 9 and its square root by 3. Thus A is correct. B ignores the scale, C incorrectly lets a translation affect spread, and D confuses variance with standard deviation.', 'For any dataset, the affine transformation y_i=ax_i+b changes its mean to a x̄+b and its variance to a² times the original variance. Standard deviation therefore becomes |a|σ. The formula holds for both population variance, with divisor n, and sample variance, with divisor n−1, as long as the same convention is used before and after transformation. Adding b shifts all observations together and leaves their pairwise distances unchanged. Multiplying by a changes every centered deviation by a; squaring explains the a² in variance. Taking the nonnegative square root explains the absolute value in standard deviation. A common error is carrying the additive constant into the spread or failing to distinguish variance from its square root.', [('A variable has standard deviation 4. Find the standard deviation of −2X+7.','It is |−2|·4=8.'),('A dataset has variance 9. Find the variance and standard deviation after y=5x−1.','Variance becomes 25·9=225 and standard deviation is 15.')])
a(5,'A device requires 4 batteries simultaneously. Each battery supplies 2 days of service. With 6 batteries, what maximum running time is possible through strategic replacement?',['2 days','3 days','4 days','5 days'],'B',['Count total battery-days and the consumption per device-day.','Split the batteries into three pairs and rotate the idle pair daily.'],'The available energy is 6·2=12 battery-days; four battery-days are needed per day, so time cannot exceed 3 days. Divide batteries into pairs P,Q,R and use P,Q on day 1, P,R on day 2, Q,R on day 3. Each battery runs exactly 2 days. Hence B is attainable; A is suboptimal and C,D violate the energy bound.', 'Resource scheduling problems need both an upper bound and a construction attaining it. If a machine consumes k identical resources at once, and resource i has capacity c_i measured in running time, then total operating time cannot exceed Σc_i/k. Equality is not guaranteed by this conservation calculation alone: each resource can contribute only while installed, and all required positions must be filled. With divisible operating periods and interchangeable batteries, balancing the usage can achieve equality when no individual capacity exceeds the proposed total time. In this question a simple pair rotation provides the required construction. Avoid counting batteries sequentially, since four are consumed simultaneously, and avoid discarding partially used batteries when replacement is allowed.', [('Three batteries, each lasting 2 hours, power a device requiring two at once. Find maximum time.','The bound is 6/2=3 hours. Use AB, AC and BC for one hour each.'),('A device uses two batteries. Four batteries each last 5 hours. Find maximum time.','Total capacity is 20 battery-hours, giving 10 hours. Use one pair for 5 hours and then the other pair for 5.')])
a(6,'Two distinct two-digit numbers are obtained by reversing each other’s digits. Their difference of squares is always divisible by which number?',['4','6','10','11'],'D',['Write the numbers as 10a+b and 10b+a.','Factor their difference of squares into their sum and difference.'],'(10a+b)²−(10b+a)²=9(a−b)·11(a+b)=99(a²−b²), always divisible by 11. For 12 and 21 the difference has magnitude 297, divisible by neither 4, 6 nor 10. Thus only D is guaranteed.', 'Decimal positional notation makes digit-reversal questions algebraic. A two-digit number with tens digit a and units digit b is 10a+b; its reversal is 10b+a. Their difference is 9(a−b) and their sum is 11(a+b). The identity u²−v²=(u−v)(u+v) then exposes universal divisibility by 99, hence by 9 and 11. A universal claim about divisibility must hold for every admissible pair, so a single counterexample is enough to reject a proposed divisor. Distinctness says a≠b; having both numbers two-digit means neither leading digit is zero. These restrictions do not change the factorization. Do not infer universal divisibility from one conveniently chosen numerical example.', [('Show that a two-digit number minus its reversal is divisible by 9.','(10a+b)−(10b+a)=9(a−b).'),('Is the sum of a two-digit number and its reversal always divisible by 11?','Yes: (10a+b)+(10b+a)=11(a+b).')])
a(7,'A loan of ₹150,000 bears compound interest at 10% per annum. It is repaid at the end of year 3. What is the total interest?',['₹45000','₹82600','₹94600','₹49650'],'D',['Compound the principal once at each year’s end.','Interest is P[(1+r)³−1], not the final balance.'],'The balance is 150000(1.1)³=199650. Interest is 199650−150000=49650. D is correct; A is the simple-interest result and B,C are neither the compounded interest nor the balance.', 'With annual compounding and no intervening payments, a principal P grows by the same factor 1+r in each year, giving balance A_n=P(1+r)^n. Interest earned or owed is A_n−P. The recurrence A_{n+1}=(1+r)A_n shows why later interest is charged on accumulated interest as well as on the original principal. Simple interest instead gives Pnr, which is smaller for positive r and more than one period. The compounding period must match the rate period; a nominal annual rate compounded m times yearly uses factor (1+r/m)^{mn}. Payments during the term require updating the outstanding balance at their actual dates. Always check whether the question asks for interest alone or principal plus interest.', [('Find compound interest on ₹1000 at 20% annually for 2 years.','The balance is 1000·1.2²=1440, so interest is ₹440.'),('A ₹5000 balance grows to ₹6050 in 2 years with annual compounding. Find the rate.','(1+r)²=6050/5000=1.21, so 1+r=1.1 and r=10%.')])
a(8,'The field ABCDE has AB=DE=AE=15 m, AB and DE perpendicular to AE. The inward edges BC and DC each make 30° with the corresponding vertical edge (see source figure). Find the perimeter.',['70 m','75 m','80 m','85 m'],'B',['AB and DE have equal lengths, so B and D lie at the same height.','Triangle BCD is isosceles with base BD=15 and base angles 60°.'],'Since BD=AE=15 and the angles CBD and BDC are 60°, triangle BCD is equilateral. Thus BC=CD=15. The boundary comprises five 15 m sides, totaling 75 m. Only B matches; A,C,D would require a different edge length.', 'When a polygon includes an inward indentation, its perimeter follows the actual boundary, including both inward edges. It is not the perimeter of its convex hull. Here parallel perpendicular edges of equal length place their lower endpoints on a line parallel to the top edge. Subtracting a 30° angle from a right angle gives the base angles of the inner triangle. Two 60° angles force the third to be 60°, and the triangle is equilateral. More generally, in an isosceles triangle with base b and base angle θ, each equal side is b/(2 cos θ). Read angle labels relative to the line indicated, rather than assuming they refer to the horizontal. Diagram scale is only illustrative.', [('A rectangular outline has width 12, vertical sides 10 and an inward equilateral triangular notch replacing its bottom side. Find perimeter.','The boundary is 12+10+10+12+12=56.'),('An isosceles triangular notch has base 10 and base angles 45°. Find each sloping side.','The equal side is 10/(2 cos45°)=5√2.')])
a(9,'A mother’s age and her daughter’s age are currently in ratio 14:1. In four years the ratio will be 16:3. How old was the mother when the daughter was born?',['26 years','28 years','30 years','32 years'],'A',['Let present ages be 14x and x.','Cross-multiply (14x+4)/(x+4)=16/3.'],'42x+12=16x+64 gives x=2. Present ages are 28 and 2, so their constant age difference is 26. The mother was 26 at birth. B is her current age; C,D do not satisfy the age equations.', 'Age ratios change as time passes, but age differences remain constant when both people age at the same rate. A current ratio m:n lets us write the ages mx and nx using one positive scale x. After t years, the ratio becomes (mx+t)/(nx+t), rather than retaining m:n. Cross-multiplication gives a linear equation for x, and the age gap is (m−n)x. To find a parent’s age at a child’s birth, subtract the child’s current age from the parent’s current age. Check positivity and verify the future ratio after solving. Do not add time to the ratio numbers directly, because those numbers represent proportional units rather than necessarily years.', [('A father and son are now in ratio 4:1. In 5 years it is 3:1. Find their current ages.','(4x+5)/(x+5)=3 gives x=10. Ages are 40 and 10.'),('A mother is 36 and daughter 6. When will their age ratio be 3:1?','(36+t)/(6+t)=3 gives 36+t=18+3t, so t=9 years.')])
a(10,'Five identical spheres of radius 1 are stacked with four mutually adjacent spheres in a square base and one touching all four above, as in the source top view. Find the total height.',['2+√2','2+√3','2+2√(2/3)','3'],'A',['Use centers: touching unit spheres have center distance 2.','The base square has side 2; its center is √2 from a vertex.'],'Base centers lie at height 1 and form a square of side 2. The top center lies above the square’s center. Its vertical separation h satisfies h²+(√2)²=2², so h=√2. Include the lower and upper radii: total height 1+√2+1=2+√2. B,C correspond to different base geometry; D incorrectly uses separation 1.', 'For identical externally tangent spheres of radius r, center-to-center distance is 2r. Packing heights are easiest to compute from center coordinates, with a final radius added at each exposed end. In a square base of four tangent spheres, the centers form a square of side 2r, and its circumradius is √2r. A fifth sphere touching all four has its center on the perpendicular through the square’s center. The Pythagorean theorem in a vertical plane gives center elevation √((2r)²−(√2r)²)=√2r. Overall height is therefore (2+√2)r. A triangular base yields another formula, so count the base spheres and use the actual diagram rather than recalling a tetrahedral packing height.', [('Repeat the square-base stack with radius 3. Find its height.','All lengths scale by 3, so height is 3(2+√2).'),('Three unit spheres form an equilateral base and a fourth touches them above. Find height.','The base circumradius is 2/√3. Center elevation is √(4−4/3)=2√(2/3); height is 2+2√(2/3).')])
a(11,'Parties A,B,C,D,E win 30,25,20,10,4 seats and independents win 9. Which statement must be incorrect?',['No party has a majority.','A and C together can form the government.','A and D, with all independents, get a majority.','An MLA from E can become Chief Minister.'],'C',['Add all seats before setting the majority threshold.','A strict majority of 98 seats needs at least 50.'],'There are 98 seats, requiring 50 for a majority. A is true because the largest party has 30. A+C=50, so B is possible. A+D+independents=30+10+9=49, so C is false. D is possible if a majority coalition supports the E member; the seat counts do not rule it out.', 'A strict majority means more than half of the total, so the minimum integer threshold is floor(N/2)+1. Exactly half is insufficient when N is even. Coalition questions require adding seats for the stated groups and comparing with that threshold. A party’s size alone need not determine which member can lead a coalition: a smaller party’s member may be accepted by a coalition with sufficient support. Distinguish a statement that must be false from one that is merely not guaranteed. Numerical data can establish or rule out a coalition’s arithmetic majority, but cannot establish political agreement. In aptitude questions, treat leadership claims as possibilities unless additional rules or commitments are explicitly supplied.', [('In a 101-seat assembly, a coalition has 50 seats. Is this a majority?','No. The threshold is floor(101/2)+1=51.'),('Parties have 28,24,18 seats in a 70-seat chamber. Can the last two form an arithmetic majority?','Yes. They total 42 and the threshold is 36.')])
a(12,'The source charts give populations in 2010→2020: A 150→160, B 1200→1400, C 180→220; GDP (billion USD): A 115→324, B 1675→2622, C 177→264. Rank countries by the increase in per capita GDP, highest first.',['A,B,C','B,A,C','B,C,A','C,A,B'],'A',['Compute GDP divided by population in each year.','Compare the differences between those ratios, not the changes in GDP alone.'],'In common units, increases are A:324/160−115/150=151/120≈1.2583; B:2622/1400−1675/1200≈0.4770; C:264/220−177/180=13/60≈0.2167. Hence A>B>C. B and C incorrectly place B first; D puts C first although its increase is smallest.', 'Per capita output is total output divided by population. Its absolute increase between two dates is G_1/P_1−G_0/P_0, not (G_1−G_0)/(P_1−P_0). Changing denominators can reverse a ranking based on total GDP growth, so both charts are needed. Units common to all countries multiply every per capita figure by the same factor and do not affect the ranking. An absolute increase differs from percentage growth, which is (G_1/P_1)/(G_0/P_0)−1. When a question simply asks for increase, calculate the absolute difference unless a percentage is specified. Read chart labels rather than estimating bar heights and retain sufficient precision when two values are close.', [('GDP rises from 200 to 300 while population rises from 100 to 120. Find per capita increase.','It changes from 2 to 2.5, an increase of 0.5 in common units.'),('GDP doubles and population rises by 25%. Find percentage growth of per capita GDP.','The per capita factor is 2/1.25=1.6, giving 60% growth.')])
a(13,'Fill the blank so the paragraph’s final sentence is accurate: “THE ABILITY TO REASON ACCURATELY IS VERY IMPORTANT, AS IS THE ABILITY TO COUNT. AS AN EXERCISE IN BOTH, LET US COUNT HOW MANY TIMES THE LETTER ‘E’ OCCURS IN THIS PARAGRAPH. THE CORRECT COUNT IS ___.”',['SIXTEEN','SEVENTEEN','EIGHTEEN','NINETEEN'],'D',['Count the existing E’s, including the quoted letter E.','The inserted number-word itself adds E’s; test consistency.'],'The fixed paragraph contains 16 E’s. SIXTEEN adds 2, giving 18≠16; SEVENTEEN adds 4, giving 20≠17; EIGHTEEN adds 3, giving 19≠18; NINETEEN adds 3, giving 19=19. Hence only D makes the statement accurate.', 'Self-referential counting problems are fixed-point problems. First count the occurrences in the fixed part of the text, excluding the blank but including any quoted occurrence of the target letter. If that count is b and a candidate word representing integer n contains e(n) target letters, consistency requires n=b+e(n). Merely counting the original paragraph is insufficient, because completing the sentence changes the object being counted. Test each proposed completion rather than assuming a number close to b is correct. The counted region is the specified paragraph, not the instructions or the option list outside it. Typography such as quotation marks and punctuation contributes no letter occurrences. A tally by words or lines helps prevent accidental omissions.', [('A sentence has 5 fixed A’s and is completed with SIX or SEVEN, neither containing A. Which works?','Neither: both give a total of 5 A’s, which agrees with neither 6 nor 7.'),('A self-counting text has 15 fixed E’s. Which of SIXTEEN and SEVENTEEN works?','SIXTEEN gives 17 and SEVENTEEN gives 19; neither equals its represented number.')])
a(14,'Datasets A and B have the same mean. Which statement must be true?',['Their observation sums are equal.','Their means of squared observations are equal.','The pooled mean equals mean(A)+mean(B).','The pooled mean equals mean(A).'],'D',['The pooled mean is weighted by dataset sizes.','If both component means are μ, the weighted mean is μ.'],'For sizes m,n and common mean μ, pooled mean=(mμ+nμ)/(m+n)=μ, proving D. A fails for A={1},B={1,1}. B fails for A={0,2},B={1,1}, whose squared means are 2 and 1. C fails whenever μ≠0 because it proposes 2μ.', 'The arithmetic mean of a nonempty finite dataset is its total divided by its number of observations. Combining datasets of sizes m and n gives the weighted mean (mμ_A+nμ_B)/(m+n). Equal component means consequently force the same pooled mean regardless of their sizes. Equal means do not force equal sums unless the sizes agree, and they do not determine spread or higher moments. In particular the second raw moment is the variance plus the square of the mean, so two datasets with equal means may have different mean squares. Universal statements should be tested with small counterexamples. Do not add means as though they were totals, and do not take an unweighted average of unequal-sized datasets unless the component means happen to agree.', [('Groups of sizes 10 and 30 have means 4 and 8. Find pooled mean.','(10·4+30·8)/40=7.'),('Two groups have mean 6 and sizes 5 and 8. What is their pooled sum?','Their sums are 30 and 48, so pooled sum is 78 and pooled mean remains 6.')])
a(15,'Among 45 people, 40 all know one another and the other 5 know nobody. Acquainted pairs hug; unacquainted pairs shake hands. How many handshakes occur?',['225','10','210','200'],'C',['Separate pairs involving one outsider from pairs of two outsiders.','There are 40·5 cross pairs and C(5,2) outsider pairs.'],'Handshakes total 40·5+C(5,2)=200+10=210. C is correct. A double-counts outsider pairs; B omits all cross pairs; D omits the ten pairs among outsiders. Equivalently C(45,2)−C(40,2)=990−780=210.', 'A handshake is an unordered pair, so N people have at most C(N,2)=N(N−1)/2 pairwise handshakes if each pair meets once. Ordered counting doubles the answer. For an acquainted subgroup of size k and N−k outsiders who know nobody, all cross pairs k(N−k) and all outsider pairs C(N−k,2) are unacquainted. Alternatively subtract the acquainted pairs C(k,2) from all pairs C(N,2). These decompositions are disjoint and exhaustive. The phrase “know one another” identifies a complete acquaintance group, whereas “know no one” includes the other outsiders. Check that every pair belongs to exactly one counted category. Multiplying each outsider’s handshake count without correcting outsider-to-outsider double counting is a common trap.', [('20 people contain 15 mutual acquaintances and 5 complete strangers. Count handshakes.','15·5+C(5,2)=75+10=85.'),('Two disjoint mutual-acquaintance groups have sizes 8 and 12. Everyone shakes hands with unfamiliar people. Count handshakes.','Only cross-group pairs are unfamiliar: 8·12=96.')])
a(16,'In a group of 7 people, 4 have exactly one sibling and 3 have exactly two siblings. Two people are selected uniformly from the group. What is the probability they are not siblings?',['5/21','16/21','3/7','4/7'],'',['Determine whether the specified siblings must belong to the group.','If every sibling is in the group, count sibling pairs from the sum of within-group degrees.'],'The source does not state that these siblings are in the group. If all are, the four one-sibling people form two pairs and the three two-sibling people form a triple, yielding 2+3=5 sibling pairs and 1−5/C(7,2)=16/21 (B). But the seven could belong to seven separate families, with all their siblings outside the group, giving probability 1. Hence no option is forced by the literal statement. A is the sibling probability under the extra closure assumption; C,D also do not follow. The conditional intended calculation is supplied without silently adding that assumption.', 'For uniform selection of two distinct people from N, every unordered pair has probability 1/C(N,2). If the within-group sibling relation forms disjoint full family groups of sizes s_j, the number of sibling pairs is ΣC(s_j,2), so the nonsibling probability is 1−ΣC(s_j,2)/C(N,2). Equivalently, the sum of each person’s number of siblings within the group counts every sibling pair twice. Total siblings in someone’s family need not equal siblings present in the sampled group. A probability calculation must specify the population and the relations within that population. Family sizes alone cannot determine the pair count when membership is incomplete; an explicit closure assumption or a list of within-group family memberships is required.', [('A group consists of complete sibling families of sizes 2,2,3. Find nonsibling probability.','There are 1+1+3=5 sibling pairs out of 21, so probability is 16/21.'),('A sample contains one person from each of five different families. Each has two siblings outside the sample. Find nonsibling probability.','All C(5,2)=10 sampled pairs are nonsiblings, so probability is 1.')],status='source_issue',note='Literal source does not say siblings are within the group. Option B follows only after adding that membership assumption.')
a(17,'Two points on a spherical globe of radius 10 have distance 25 units. Under uniform expansion to radius 50, what is their distance?',['75','125','150','625'],'B',['Uniform expansion multiplies every length by the radius ratio.','The scale factor is 50/10=5.'],'The surface distance becomes 5·25=125, so B is correct. A,C use wrong linear factors; D uses the area factor 25. The initial 25 exceeds diameter 20, so the stated distance must be along the globe’s surface, consistent with arc length rθ.', 'A uniform geometric dilation with factor k multiplies every length by k, every area by k² and every volume by k³. On a sphere, an arc subtending a fixed central angle θ has length rθ, so expanding the radius preserves θ and scales arc length linearly. Chord lengths also scale linearly, although the distance in this source cannot be a chord because it exceeds the original diameter. The question’s common units are retained; there is no unit-conversion factor. Distinguish the dimension of the requested quantity before using a scaling law. A radius change from r to R gives length factor R/r, not an additive change R−r or a squared factor appropriate to surface area.', [('A sphere expands from radius 3 to 12. A surface arc was 7 long. Find its new length.','The factor is 4, so the arc becomes 28.'),('A globe’s radius doubles. How do its surface area and volume change?','The surface area multiplies by 2²=4 and volume by 2³=8.')])
a(18,'Which source diagram correctly relates INSECTS, BIRDS, EXTINCT ANIMALS and PEACOCKS? The source provides diagrams A–D.',['Diagram A: one nested pair, a third set overlapping the outer set, and a fourth isolated set.','Diagram B: one isolated nested pair and a separate overlapping pair.','Diagram C: peacocks inside birds; extinct animals overlap birds and insects; birds and insects are disjoint.','Diagram D: two overlapping outer sets, each containing an overlapping inner set.'],'C',['Peacocks are a subset of birds, while birds and insects are disjoint.','Extinct animals include members of both birds and insects; peacocks belong outside the extinct part.'],'Diagram C can label its left large set birds, inner set peacocks, middle overlapping set extinct animals, and right set insects. It displays the required subset and intersections. A leaves one category isolated from extinct animals; B has no set overlapping both birds and insects; D forces extra subset/overlap relations between the unrelated categories. Therefore C is the appropriate source diagram.', 'Euler diagrams depict subset relationships by containment, disjointness by separation, and shared members by overlapping regions. A category such as extinct animals cuts across biological groups rather than sitting entirely within birds or insects. A specific group such as peacocks sits within birds, whereas insects and birds are separate animal categories. To match an unlabeled diagram, assign the most restrictive relationship first, usually the nested subset, and then check every remaining overlap. A drawing with the right number of circles may still impose a false intersection or omit a required one. The existence of an overlap means at least some common members, not that all members of either set belong to the other. Use the source diagram to identify the option, rather than silently replacing its geometry.', [('Describe the sets mammals, dogs and fish.','Dogs lie within mammals; fish are disjoint from mammals and hence from dogs.'),('Describe students, athletes and tennis players if every tennis player is an athlete and some students play tennis.','Tennis players lie within athletes, and the students set intersects that subset; students may also include nonathletes.')])
a(19,'A boy can escape through a window of size at least 4 feet. There are 28 windows of sizes 2,3,4,5 feet, with counts proportional to their sizes. How many are usable?',['2','9','10','18'],'D',['Write counts as 2k,3k,4k,5k.','The total ratio weight is 14; include both size 4 and size 5.'],'14k=28 gives k=2. The counts are 4,6,8,10, and the usable number is 8+10=18. D is correct. A is the scale factor, B the usable ratio weight before scaling, and C counts only the 5-foot windows.', 'When category frequencies are proportional to given positive weights r_i, write them as kr_i and determine k from N=kΣr_i. A threshold condition then selects the categories whose measurements satisfy it, and their count is k times the sum of their weights. “At least” includes the boundary value; thus a threshold of four includes windows of sizes four and five. Proportionality refers to counts here, not to total window area or total width. Objects are indivisible, so the calculated frequencies should be nonnegative integers. Keeping ratio weights and actual counts in separate steps prevents the error of reporting an unscaled weight sum. Check that all category counts add back to the stated total.', [('30 objects have sizes 1,2,3 and counts proportional to size. How many have size at least 2?','k=30/6=5; count is 5(2+3)=25.'),('There are 40 windows of sizes 1,3,4, with counts proportional to size. How many exceed size 3?','k=40/8=5. Only size 4 qualifies, giving 4·5=20.')])
a(20,'A 10-question examination awards +2 for correct, −1 for incorrect and 0 for unattempted answers. Which score is impossible?',['−9','−7','17','19'],'D',['Let c,w,u be nonnegative integers summing to 10.','The score is 2c−w; examine the largest scores.'],'−9 occurs with (c,w,u)=(0,9,1); −7 with (0,7,3); 17 with (9,1,0). For 19, c≤9 implies score≤18, while c=10 forces w=u=0 and score20. Thus D alone is impossible.', 'Discrete scoring problems impose both a linear equation and integer feasibility. With correct count c, wrong count w and unattempted count u, require c+w+u=N and c,w,u≥0 integers. A score ac−bw is possible only when these constraints have a solution. Bounds such as −bN≤score≤aN are necessary but not sufficient; gaps can occur near the maximum because replacing a correct answer with an unattempted one loses a marks, and replacing it with a wrong one loses a+b. Relative to the all-correct score aN, the deficit is au+(a+b)w. This alternate formulation often proves impossibility quickly. Verify possible choices by explicit counts rather than relying only on interval bounds.', [('Five questions award +3,−1,0. Is score14 possible?','Maximum15 has deficit1, but every change loses 3 or4. Thus14 is impossible.'),('Ten questions award +2,−1,0. Give a configuration scoring15.','Eight correct, one wrong and one unattempted give16−1=15.')])
